+# Script craft and the one discovery root the specification itself teaches
+
+source: S236 — https://agentskills.io/skill-creation/using-scripts.md — `# One-off commands` and the opening paragraph — fetched as `.md` 12 078 B on 2026-10-23
+source: S237 — https://agentskills.io/skill-creation/quickstart.md — `# Prerequisites` and `# Create the skill` — fetched as `.md` 4 080 B on 2026-10-23
+verified-at: 2026-10-23
+recheck: 90d — re-fetch both `.md` pages and confirm the one-off-command list and the `.agents/skills/` default are unchanged (ran 2026-10-23)
+ground: the lane's craft-rules item — the open specification's own authoring guidance, which the lane held only for descriptions and best practices, and the one discovery root the specification endorses by example.
+
+**The script half of a skill, stated normatively.** The specification's own
+script-craft page (S236) is the last unheld page of the `skill-creation/` set, and it
+covers what the lane's other craft sources do not: the deterministic half of a skill.
+"Skills can instruct agents to run shell commands and bundle reusable scripts in a
+`scripts/` directory." The rules that follow are practical and checkable: when an
+existing package already does what you need, reference it directly and skip the
+`scripts/` directory — the page's worked examples are `uvx ruff@0.8.0 check .`,
+`pipx run 'black==24.10.0' .`, `npx eslint@9 --fix .` and `bunx`. Pin the version
+(`npx package@version`) for reproducibility. And design a script's interface for an
+*agent* to call, not for a human — which is the same principle the lane already has
+from TRAE's guide ("the skill itself does not understand your code logic, it only
+perceives input and output"), now stated by the format maintainers.
+
+**The discovery root the specification teaches by example.** The quickstart (S237)
+says: "VS Code looks for skills in `.agents/skills/` by default". That is worth
+recording carefully, because the lane has met `.agents/skills/` in **four** vendor
+surfaces — TRAE (behind an explicit toggle, with `.trae/skills/` winning a name
+collision), Kimi (as a discovery group), opencode (as one of six roots across three
+compatibility tiers), and Qwen Code (as a cross-vendor root read without
+configuration) — and had never established it as the *specification's* position. Now
+it is: the specification's own tutorial teaches `.agents/skills/` as the default
+location, and the same tutorial's skill is written to travel — "the same skill works
+in any compatible agent, including Claude Code and OpenAI Codex". The cross-vendor
+root is therefore the specification's own answer to its own portability problem, not
+a vendor convenience that happened to spread.
+
+**The tutorial's description, as a worked example.** The quickstart's skill declares
+`description: Roll dice using a random number generator. Use when asked to roll a die
+(d6, d20, etc.), roll dice, or generate a random dice roll.` — one sentence of
+capability, then a list of the phrases a user might actually type. That is the
+specification's own instance of the rule its `optimizing-descriptions` page states:
+describe the function, then list the contexts, including phrasings the user would
+not connect to the domain name.
